<!-- cell: title-page id="d62ee94f-e222-47e6-a59c-60cfc1b32bbf" title="The Hands of St Aldric's" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="d17b9ab0-c9ff-499f-aba2-7d7669825790" -->

On Monday morning the clock on the tower of St Aldric's said nothing at all. Its face still hung pale and round above the village green, but both of its gilded hands were gone.

By ten o'clock Little Dunmore had decided it was the Pratt boys. Detective Inspector Ruth Abara, sent forty minutes out for what the desk sergeant called a nice easy one, listened to all of it and wrote none of it down.

Reverend Hollis met her at the lych gate, a tall, gentle man with soil under his fingernails and his cardigan buttoned wrong. "A terrible thing," he said, not meeting her eye. "Those hands were three hundred years old."

The tower stair was narrow and dusty, every step furred grey, except the last flight to the clock chamber, which had been swept so clean the stone shone. Up there, the bolts that had held the hands had been undone with care, by someone with the right spanner and plenty of time.

"Thieves don't sweep up after themselves," said Abara, and went back down to look at the vicar instead of the clock.

In the vestry a bucket stood beneath a brown stain in the ceiling, half full of rainwater. On the desk beside it lay an invoice from a roofing firm in Cambridge, stamped PAID IN FULL and dated Saturday.

"The diocese said the roof could wait until spring," said Hollis quietly, looking at the bucket. "It couldn't. There's a family of five who sit under that stain every Sunday."

"Where did the money come from, Reverend?" asked Abara gently.

He sat down heavily. "A dealer in Norwich. He said gilt hands of that age were worth four thousand pounds to the right collector. I swept the stairs because I couldn't bear to leave a mess in God's house. I could steal from it, but I couldn't leave it dirty."

Abara closed her notebook. She had what she had come for, and no wish to put a vicar in a cell. "You'll tell the parish council yourself, tonight," she said, "and the Pratt boys will get an apology from the pulpit."

A month later the clock of St Aldric's had new hands, carved from oak and painted gold by the bell-ringers. They were slightly crooked, and they kept perfect time.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5388d156-3ded-4b0c-8972-fd4e7e30569a" title="Detective Inspector Ruth Abara" words="46-74, 165-185, 203-223, 254-263, 314-354" -->

A patient, unhurried detective who ignores gossip and reads people from small things — a swept step, a bucket, a look away. She wants the truth, and once she has it she prefers justice to arrest.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="51208287-dcff-4fa7-90e7-fb764208a494" title="Reverend Hollis" words="75-112, 224-253, 264-313" -->

The gentle, conscientious vicar of St Aldric's, more afraid of a family getting wet than of breaking the law. He steals the clock's hands to mend the roof, and cannot help sweeping up after himself.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="ffc26a3e-c997-414c-bc07-a69d817dfc38" title="St Aldric's church" words="0-33, 113-140, 186-202, 224-253, 355-383" -->

The village church of Little Dunmore: its silent clock above the green, its dusty tower stair, and its leaking vestry with the family who sit under the stain every Sunday.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="9feb238e-894a-424c-93b0-a1313cf34fed" title="Theft" words="0-33, 34-45, 97-112, 113-164, 203-223, 264-287, 335-354" -->

The theft of the clock's three-hundred-year-old gilded hands: unbolted with care, sold to a dealer in Norwich, blamed on the Pratt boys, and confessed.

<!-- cell: plot id="8fa8e565-50cb-4dbd-b07b-0cd7bd97c97d" title="The stolen hands" words="0-33, 34-45, 113-140, 165-185, 203-223, 254-263, 264-313, 335-354, 355-383" -->

The clock's hands vanish and the village blames the Pratt boys; Abara reads the swept stair and the paid roofing invoice, the vicar confesses, and the clock gets new oak hands.
